remarkably individuals showed an average  decrease in biological age of eight  years  so hello and welcome to the cheeky  science show where in this video we'll  break down a recent paper that has given  us a first look at some data of human  patients taking regiment and  alpha-ketoglutarate-based formulation  whereby they assess their biological age  by using the dna methylation clock  so in this video we'll break this paper  down completely address exactly what the  data shows what it doesn't show and what  i think the take-homes are so firstly  what is alpha-ketoglutarate  well alpha-ketoglutarate otherwise known  as akg  my students would probably know very  well as being a key intermediate in the  krebs cycle which is important for  aerobic respiration  so this is important for burning glucose  fats and proteins to generate the  reducing power to make atp the energy  source of the cell  however akg is more than just a  component of the krebs cycle  it can also be used by the body as a  precursor to amino acid production amino  acids being the building blocks of  different proteins  as well as acting as a cofactor for  different enzymes in the body  it was previously shown in a study that  akg supplementation increases the  lifespan of the worm c elegans and i  reported on a study that came out last  year showing that the calcium salt of  akg extended both the lifespan and  health span of mice  so given that akg is generally  recognized as safe  the company that sponsored this latter  study ponce de leon health wanted to  test it for human aging so this brings  us on to this recent publication that  has already caused a lot of excitement  on different social media platforms  so what exactly did they do  well in the study they had the  participants taking regiment which is a  product that's currently being sold by  the company that sponsored the previous  research that have a sustained released  version of a calcium salt of  alpha-ketoglutarate  and that source of akg is combined with  a specific vitamin depending on the sex  of the participants  so for females they take it along with  vitamin d and for males they take it  with vitamin a  and so the dose that they were taking  were two tablets per day which meant  that one dose contained one gram of  calcium alpha-ketoglutarate  in terms of who and how many people were  in this study you can see the data  summarized here so in total there are 42  participants one-third but females and  two-thirds were males and their ages  ranged from 41 to 79 years with a mean  age of 63  all were healthy without any chronic  medical conditions  and each participant was taking regiment  on average for seven months  so what were they assessing in the study  well the only measurement that they took  was dna methylation that was detected by  sequencing once they took some saliva  samples from the participants at  baseline so before they started taking  the product and on average after seven  months after the participants were  taking the measurements  so what does this mean well dna  methylation is a epigenetic mark so it's  a mark that's found on top of your dna  sequence  in this case it refers to a cytosine  residue so cytosine being one of the  four bases found in dna that can get  methylated and so you can use a  sequencing approach to detect either the  presence or absence of methylation and  then what's been done more recently and  pioneered particularly by steve holvath  is to use these measurements and predict  based on whether or not there is a  methylation mark or not  the chronological age of the participant  and so this kind of inferred  chronological age is also referred to as  a biological age  there are many different types of  epigenetic clocks currently available  but the one that they used in this paper  is based on this true age prediction  model and that only needs nine of these  methylation sites  and so by having less sites that also  does make it a bit cheaper for them to  perform this test  now as you can see in this figure here  they first tested whether or not this  correlated with the participant's  chronological age and you can see a  positive correlation too and so  effectively here they were validating  the use of this model  so what happened after they took akg  well on average they saw that the dna  methylation age decreased by 8.31 years  by the end of the period and you can see  the data summarized in this figure here  and so the y axis here is showing  chronological age minus the true age  epigenetic predicted age so if the  number is bigger than zero then the  predicted biological age is younger than  your actual chronological age  so in general most of the lines trend up  between before and after suggesting that  the biological age has reduced following  the treatment  you'll notice that not all the lines  trend up and the amount of change also  varies but on average this was 8.31  years  now importantly because the relevant  supplement varies between males and  females they also look specifically at  the sex-specific changes and so in both  cases they also do see a decrease in the  epigenetic age  suggesting that it's more likely to be  the akg than the fertility differences  a few other things worth pointing out  with this data is that only two  individuals actually showed a slight  increase in their political age one male  and one female  and also it's interesting to note that  even though some of the participants  were taking this supplement for  different time periods there was no  correlation with the magnitude of the  effect  and so this may suggest that over a  longer period of time the benefits may  be saturated with supplementation  so this seems like exciting news so is  it time to all start taking akg well  whilst the data hit is very exciting i  have to say there are a few points i  want to make  firstly it's important to note that this  is not placebo controlled so all the  participants here were taking akg and  they knew that they were taking it  and so some of this benefit could  literally just be due to placebo  secondly it was a short-term study and  it would still be important to  characterize long-term effects of taking  the supplements  but another really important thing to  stress is that the only thing that they  measured was this dna methylation age  there are no other clinical markers  given in this publication neither is  there any functional measurements  and so it would be hard to say exactly  what akg could be doing to the body and  whether this actually refers as more  functional aspects of aging  and so this brings me on to more talking  about this dna methylation because  firstly it would have been interested to  at least validate this with other  methylation clocks and also because at  the moment the association between these  different sites of methylation and the  aging process are still a bit unclear  and then lastly i guess it's worth  mentioning that the company behind this  paper are trying to sell their products  commercially and say i guess that's kind  of a big disclaimer and the data that  they might present  so all in all whilst i think this data  is very encouraging i think it's  important to realize that this paper  doesn't provide definitive proof of the  so-called fountain of youth but i will  keep you posted as we learn more  information  and whether or not we can see this data  translated through to randomized  controlled clinical trials  so with that i hope you plan to make in  this video thank you to my patreon  supporters and thank you for listening  you
 

